# Build & Install Workflow

## User shorthand

| Shorthand | Meaning | Agent action |
|-----------|---------|--------------|
| **bi** | build + install | `app\build-APK.bat install` |
| **build** / compile check | release APK only | `app\build-APK.bat` |

Treat **bi**, **build install**, and **build and install** as equivalent.

## Canonical build script — always use this

**Do not** run raw `gradlew` from the agent unless `build-APK.bat` is unavailable.
**Do not** pipe Gradle output to `Select-Object -Last N` or `Select-String` when the
user is waiting — that hides progress and looks frozen.

```
app\build-APK.bat           REM build only — live log
app\build-APK.bat install   REM build + adb install (bi)
```

`build-APK.bat` sets `GRADLE_USER_HOME=C:\gradle-hm` (short path — avoids Windows
260-char CMake failures), checks JDK via `check-java-for-android.bat`, and passes
`--console=plain` so Gradle streams task lines immediately.

| Script | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **`build-APK.bat`** | Release APK for phone testing (this workflow) |
| **`build-dev.bat`** | Debug dev client + Metro — daily JS work, not release |
| **`build-release.bat`** | Legacy; prefer `build-APK.bat` |
| **`publish-apk-vps.bat`** | Build (if needed) + upload APK to VPS |

## Default behaviour after code changes

**Build for compilation check only** — do not install unless the user asks.

```
app\build-APK.bat
```

Install only when user says **bi** / install / test on phone:

```
app\build-APK.bat install
```

## When to install
- User explicitly says: "install", **"bi"**, "build install", "test on phone", "let me try it", "rebuild and install"
- After a bug fix the user reported from a device screenshot
- At the end of a feature batch when the user asks to verify

## When NOT to install automatically
- After every small fix (wastes 30–40 seconds per iteration)
- After a compilation-only change (import cleanup, type fix, dead code removal)
- When the user just asked to "rebuild" without mentioning the phone

## Commit policy — NEVER commit before phone test

**Do NOT `git commit` or `git push` after a feature build until the user explicitly
confirms they tested the APK on their phone and it works.**

Trigger words that mean "ok to commit":
- "commit", "push", "lgtm", "looks good", "works", "it's working", "ship it"

Trigger words that mean "NOT ok to commit yet":
- "build", "compile check", "let me test", "install" — wait for test result first

```
# ✅ Correct order
1. build-APK.bat  →  2. install (when asked)  →  3. User tests  →  4. User says "commit"  →  5. git commit & push

# ❌ Never do
Build APK  →  git commit  (skips phone test)
```

## Agent fallback (PowerShell — no && separator)

If the batch file cannot be used, mirror its flags exactly:

```powershell
$env:GRADLE_USER_HOME = "C:\gradle-hm"
Set-Location "c:\projects\healthings-medilab\app\android"
.\gradlew.bat assembleRelease --no-build-cache --console=plain
```

Install (bi only):

```powershell
adb install -r "c:\projects\healthings-medilab\app\android\app\build\outputs\apk\release\app-release.apk"
```

Agent-only quick tail when user is **not** watching: last 12 lines is OK.

## Clean build (only when native modules / codegen errors)

```powershell
$env:GRADLE_USER_HOME = "C:\gradle-hm"
Set-Location "c:\projects\healthings-medilab\app\android"
.\gradlew.bat assembleRelease --no-build-cache --console=plain
```

Avoid `gradlew clean` unless codegen dirs are broken — see prior notes in `build-dev.bat`.
